---

google glass without cameras and no lenses - instead, tiny robot arms that
extend and rotate little panels, and a laser or similar writes single photons
of light in a particular pattern that is determined beyind the aperture of the
projector with repositioned panels and beyond the panels on the interior of the
device is a mirror cylinder section that redirects the light in a reasonable
shape and points it outward. You should be able to display the light
intermittently, otherwise it'll collect heat. Radiate light outward, and only
turn it on when the user is looking at that spot. Meaning... multiple emitters
per UI display? maybe... they can radiate heat with a series of air pumps and
no fans

---

(on single photons being too few for the eye to see:)

they can be stored in the rotating internal mirror cylinder, only letting a
certain amount out at a time as the aperture rotates.

(on the panels:)

not robot arms drawing. the laser's aperture is made up of long "tabs" that
can spand in and out of the aperture. Each part of them has "slots" that can
be opened or not. they overlap each other in the long passage from the lasers,
which are at the very back of the device which wraps around and rests like a
crown on your head. same color as your hair, but not made out of hair that'd
be weird lookin'. anyway the tab projection rotates on an axis toward or away
from the aperture, and at different points along it it opens slots in it's
length to let the laser, which is at the back of the head by your temple, is
allowed through. there are multiple of these, and they can rotate around the
fiber-optic style solid glass plus obscuringment section. It should be
mirrored internally so the whole thing is radiating warmth, and storing
photons. this is... wildly impractical because it would change optical
indexes when it passed from one material to the material that the tab was
immersed in, which is why it has to be a seamless hydrogel or liquid.

(on lenses:)

only the photons emitted from the laser can be cast out, but the ones that get
redirected from tabs just occupy space and prevent the flow down the center
from being disrupted. unless the scene changes, and then it takes a second as
the tabs re-adjust. the faster, the more responsive. room for mechanical
improvement.

(on heat:)

when it's hot, we can have heatsinks that layer on your hair and radiate
warmth. you can let them expand up a bit, flaring out, to radiate sweat
warmth.

(on where the light goes:)

out of the glasses toward the world, but intercepted by one of the panels the
user has attached to a hydraulic arm that pushes directly out and pulls closer
according to their specification. The entire arm rotates around it's axis as
well, to adjust the panels slightly. input decisions, rotate this arm
slightly, it'll change the display output to reflect your moderation, and the
panel doesn't rotate it just curves around. the user can also use a thumbstick
installed in their pocket, or a switchable-on-and-off part of their sleeve or
wrist-cuffs on a sweatshirt, sweater, or other jacket style outerwear.

oh also... on the arms... there's two separate rotating inputs, a forward and
a back, and there's also the one up by your ears... so three on each side
actually. also a physical button is an input A or B, no taps those can be
easily triggered.

(on how the device knows where to draw, with no camera:)

your inputs determine what gets shown.

